window.DIGEST.foryou.push(...[{"id": "2112751346948411683", "url": "https://x.com/David_Kasten/status/2112751346948411683", "text": "You describe a major policy rollout that would affect US law nationwide, and preempt the laws of the state you're resident in, as merely \"rhetoric or policy communication that wasn’t carefully written or resulting from fast-changing circumstances not propagating from one team to another fast enough.\"\n\nI doubt that framing and diagnosis of root causes.  But let's grant it the sake of argument.\n\nWhat are you doing therefore to ensure that your government affairs team learns how to pay attention to, and get in sync with, the concerns of Anthropic technical staff broadly?  Where is your blameless postmortem to prevent this failure from happening again on matters of national security policy consequence?", "name": "dave kasten", "handle": "David_Kasten", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1589811166670381057/Hckp4GJt_200x200.jpg", "likes": 74, "rts": 6, "replies": 1, "views": 7372, "ts": 1792554148, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2112732230124818891", "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.64, "thread": [{"id": "2112732230124818891", "url": "https://x.com/DarioAmodei/status/2112732230124818891", "text": "Thank you for this, @_NathanCalvin. I appreciate and am a fan of the investigative work you’ve done on behalf of AI safety and believe your comments are coming from a constructive, honest place, and that it benefits the public to have people whose job is to scrutinize the actions of frontier AI companies. I think something would be wrong if companies of the size of Anthropic didn’t get harshly criticized basically every day; strong antibodies against corporate power are a healthy feature of a free society, and especially so when issues of the gravity of the one we’re facing are at play. I also do agree that some of the examples you cite were mistakes by Anthropic.\n\nThat said, I disagree with the thrust of your post. At the core, a good fraction of the things you criticize are consequences of Anthropic’s basic theory of change that we have held for many years. Our strategy has always been what I’ve called the “race to the top” — by showing how it’s possible to build the technology responsibly, we can raise the bar for the entire industry. The forces that drive the race to the top are hugely more effective if the company practicing them is at the frontier; that’s one lesson I’ve learned repeatedly. And looking at that strategy as a whole, I’m proud of our track record and stand by it.\n\nI believe that if we take a list of things that Anthropic did in the past year that were pro-safety and commercially costly to us, they far outweigh the list you’ve made, while at the same time I also disagree with some of the items on your list. Here are some things Anthropic has done, in the same period you’ve cited (the last year), which were very costly to us commercially while having strong safety benefits:\n\n1. We played a key role in SB53 (and other state laws), at a time when doing so was politically perilous to the company and AI regulation was very unpopular. We in fact argued for a stronger version of SB53 than was ultimately passed; there were a lot of stakeholders and we were not the limiting factor in the strength of the bill that was passed.\n\n2. We have very strong biological safeguards on our models, because we’ve seen concerning results in bio uplift trials and even seen and blocked potential attempts to misuse our models for bioweapons (as seen in our threat intelligence report from a few weeks ago). This is a very serious threat that we want to have zero tolerance for. But having very low false negatives means having significant false positives (despite months of effort on our part to improve the classifiers), enough so that biologists joke about how restrictive our classifiers are, and competitors sometimes sell against us by touting their more permissive bio capabilities. This has been very painful to us in an area where we care a lot about supporting an ecosystem of Claude users (biomedical science; and it is particularly frustrating to me as a biologist), but it’s the right thing to do and we intend to keep doing it.\n\n3. Similarly, we first had Mythos-class models in February but didn’t make them generally available until June (when we had stronger cyber safeguards in place). We only offered them to a limited set of partners in order to focus on cyberdefense. It would have been much more economically rational to simply release the model to everyone. We likely sacrificed a significant chunk of a several-month model lead. But because of this, many companies secured their cyber infrastructure before the deluge of widely available cyber-capable models. Of course there are ways it could have gone better or been run better, but this was what we were aiming to do.\n\n4. I advocated forcefully and publicly, in my essay Policy on the AI Exponential in June of this year, for the government to have the power to block model releases. Back then this was still outside or maybe on the edge of the Overton window, though I understand the window has moved since then. We were attacked intensely for this.\n\n5. I don’t want to re-ignite conflict, but the events of February 2026 are a strong example. It is clear that our public stance could have seriously damaged the company’s future.\n\n6. We’ve continued to advocate for export controls on chips and chipmaking equipment to China, because we think it allows democracies to negotiate from a position of strength on AI safety agreements. We’ve done this despite problems it’s created for us with industry partners who hold very different views and on whom we are very dependent.\n\n7. As far as I’m aware we were the first, and still only, frontier company to restrict the sale of AI services to PRC-controlled companies, including those operating through third countries (otherwise a major loophole). We’ve therefore forfeited significant short-term business to prevent fueling AI platforms and applications that would help the CCP’s military and intelligence services. We are also cracking down on distillation; generally, distillation is not a major threat to us commercially, but like chip exports it has concerning national security and safety implications, so we expend significant energy and company resources to address it.\n\n8. As you mentioned, we’ve put out a lot of reports, announcements, long model cards, public advocacy, etc, all trying to inform the public about the risks of AI. These involve a level of detail and effort that would be implausible for a purely profit-maximizing company.\n\n9. Just this week, we announced we were putting 5% (and possibly more over time) of the company into a public benefit fund.\n\nAll of these decisions came with very substantial costs to us. To be fair, those that involved holding back or restricting access to frontier capabilities also had a broader cost — for example, the public might otherwise have benefitted from faster progress in biology, so I am not saying that our decisions were without tradeoffs — but they were not driven by profit-seeking.\n\nFor each decision, you can come up with ulterior motives that could have driven us — I think it is a general truth that any decision, on any topic, can always be portrayed as a nefarious scheme. But most of those end up being pretty farfetched or don’t fully make sense if you actually examine them closely. For example, it is deeply implausible that it would be a shrewd business move to block a large swath of research biology in order to do the work ourselves and compete with our customers; we expect the benefit to the ecosystem to greatly exceed the work Anthropic does itself, and for the two to complement each other. It is not in Anthropic’s interest to be perceived as unfriendly to biologists. Our Life Sciences Verification Program, which gives vetted biology professionals access to our models, also refutes this idea. \n\nOf course, a different set of people will see this and make a different criticism: that Anthropic shouldn't be making decisions about who gets access to what in the first place. I agree, in the sense that ideally the government would be making these decisions, and that’s why we advocate for regulation. But for now we are forced to make them, and enabling bioterrorism isn’t a better option.\n\nI think that even if I conceded every item in your list, I would happily hold up mine next to it; I don’t think it’s a close call. That is of course partly subjective and different things are important to different people. Maybe one way you could object is that several of the items above relate to AI misuse and not loss-of-control; if you think the latter is much more important than the former, that might change your calculus. But this is not Anthropic’s view — we view both as serious issues. You can also disagree with our decisions, as many have, but they are consistent.\n\nBut I also disagree with at least some of your list. Several items don’t represent a deviation from Anthropic’s strategy or values at all, once we postulate the “race to the top” strategy that requires being a frontier AI company. Your items 1-4 are all simply examples of what it means to compete at the frontier, and we stand by them, both in the sense that they are transparently ordinary things to do as part of that strategy, and that we stand by the strategy as a whole. \n\nTo be fair, there are important questions worth confronting (and that we are confronting) about what to do when the AI-driven acceleration gets fast enough that it is hard for safety to keep up. We are nearing that point and that’s what’s driving some of our thinking about “pacing.” But 1-4 are actions taken before that point and should be perfectly expected based on Anthropic’s strategy as a company. Of course it is possible to disagree with that strategy, but that’s a more fundamental disagreement that you could have had any time over the last few years.\n\nI agree we could have handled your items 7, 8, and 9 better. All fall into the category of “rhetoric or policy communication that wasn’t carefully written or resulting from fast-changing circumstances not propagating from one team to another fast enough.” This kind of thing happens a lot in large organizations (Anthropic is over 5,000 people), and doesn’t even necessarily represent a failure or mistake of any single person. I am of course the CEO, and I take responsibility. The volume of communication from Anthropic is enormous and if these are the worst examples, I think we’re frankly doing pretty well, though I will continue to work hard to make sure we do even better.\n\nYour 5th item (the change to our RSP) is the most substantive item, and people can debate this, but I think that the old, more rigid RSP created a lot of situations where we were technically required to do something that required a lot of effort but in retrospect wasn’t very important for safety, as well as not covering some of the most important things. Our current RSP’s focus on transparency brought things like risk reports and roadmaps, and its emphasis on independent external review laid the groundwork for even more intensive embedded evaluation, all of which I think have been valuable in driving a race to the top. In the original announcement in September 2023, we described the RSP as an “early iteration that we [would] build on” and said that “rapid iteration…will almost certainly be necessary.” I understand that changing plans is always a move that creates justifiable scrutiny — I just don’t think we ever represented that we wouldn’t do so.\n\nRelating to your 6th item (having an FCF in addition to our RSP), I know our intent is to meet the letter and spirit of our regulatory obligations (via our FCF) while also remaining able to experiment with more novel approaches to risk management that go beyond what the law currently requires (via our RSP). I promise to look into it further, as it’s a complex issue and it is possible we are not weighing the considerations in the right way.\n\nOverall, although Anthropic has made plenty of mistakes as an organization — including some of the items on your list — I feel that we have repeatedly taken serious and substantive actions counter to our bottom line and that there is a significant negativity bias distorting assessments like the one you are making above. We’ll always try to do better but, again, I am extremely proud of our track record and stand by it. I will have more to say soon about how we are going to further live our values in this moment of rapid change.", "name": "Dario Amodei", "handle": "DarioAmodei", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2015835742577012736/uOwdzrEz_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1884, "rts": 144, "replies": 262, "views": 264787, "ts": 1792549590, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112295219765137570", "url": "https://x.com/_NathanCalvin/status/2112295219765137570", "text": "Thank you for taking the time to respond thoroughly on a weekend. I realize that drafting legislation is difficult and some degree of iteration and response to feedback is inevitable and appropriate.\n\nHowever, I feel like your response is still not really engaging with the heart of my concern about Anthropic's behavior here, and it makes me think that these sorts of problems and tendencies  will only resurface moving forward. I also think that now might be a good time to take a broader step back as to where we find ourselves and how Anthropic sees its role, and how external folks who care about AI safety like myself increasingly see Anthropic.\n\n(Fair warning: this response is a bit long)\n\nTo explain what I mean (and at the risk of being a bit esoteric), it’s worth asking the question: what is Anthropic? How do its leaders and employees think of Anthropic’s role? Is that conception matched by how it actually behaves and impacts the world?\n\nOne answer is an extremely large tech company that exists to make its employees and investors mind-bogglingly wealthy. Anthropic is sometimes described as a startup, but if Anthropic successfully IPOs at 2 trillion dollars (a number it has been reportedly targeting), it would be more valuable than Meta - which is worth 1.8 trillion.\n\nAnother answer is an AI safety organization, whose core values are those of the sort laid out in the 2023 essay from Anthropic’s co-founders entitled “Core views on AI safety: When, why, what, and how.” In that essay they said that if we were in a world where alignment was hard (and that without strong evidence to the contrary, we should assume this is the scenario we are in):\n\n “Anthropic’s role will be to provide as much evidence as possible that AI safety techniques cannot prevent serious or catastrophic safety risks from advanced AI, and to sound the alarm so that the world’s institutions can channel collective effort towards preventing the development of dangerous AIs. If we’re in a “near-pessimistic” scenario, this could instead involve channeling our collective efforts towards AI safety research and halting AI progress in the meantime. Indications that we are in a pessimistic or near-pessimistic scenario may be sudden and hard to spot. We should therefore always act under the assumption that we still may be in such a scenario unless we have sufficient evidence that we are not.”\n\nThere have been more recent articulations of this version of Anthropic, for instance in Dario’s recent essay warning: \n\n“We must slow the pace at which we improve the capabilities of AI models… it’s my worry that in 6–12 months such a swarm could be capable of taking over the entire internet with a persistent botnet…  incidents have happened across the industry, including at Anthropic, and I believe it’s incumbent on every frontier AI company to act as if OAI-HF had happened to them… more intelligent models are more capable of deceiving tests, and thus may appear aligned while having serious problems that go undetected…”\n\nA third view is that Anthropic exists not as a normal profit motivated company, or an AI safety organization seeking to help raise the alarm and solve collective action problems associated with AI development, but instead as an organization primarily concerned with creating superhuman AI capabilities before anyone else, in particular through creating a process whereby the current generation of Anthropic’s AI systems can autonomously build their successors, and those successors build their successors, and so on. \n\nThe motivations for wanting to win this race may be manifold - maybe for the pure profit of Answer 1, but perhaps also because Anthropic believes that the best way to make the world better is for them to win this race instead of their more reckless colleagues; or, because of normal human pride and ambition. But the results (and risks, which Anthropic claims existentially threaten every single human being on earth) are the same.\n\nAnthropic employees and leaders may seek to answer this question on what Anthropic is by introspecting on their own intentions and those of their colleagues. But those of us outside the organization have more limited tools available to us - we can only see what Anthropic and its employees say and do, not how it feels or looks on the inside.\n\nWhen I look at many notable actions that Anthropic has taken over the past year, that evidence seems most consistent to me with the view that Anthropic is primarily trying to win the race to super intelligence, and that other considerations (including safety) are fundamentally secondary. These actions include: \n\n1. Announced plans to build an in-house custom silicon chip team to accelerate the inference speeds that Claude operates at.\n\n2. Inked deals for more than $500 billion in compute (according to the Information) to further accelerate AI development.\n\n3. Increased the percentage of AI development tasks that Claude “leads” from 0% in March of this year to 26% August (what is it in October?)\n\n4. Spent a massive percent of compute on internal AI R&D to accelerate development, including use of internal models far better than are available to the public.\n\n5. Switched to Responsible Scaling Policy V3, which abandons its unilateral commitments to slow or stop development if Anthropic is unable to provide sufficient risk assurance.\n\n6. Was the first company to create an alternative weaker “frontier compliance framework” to supplement its RSP, which contains no binding provisions whatsoever on internal deployments.\n\n7. Wrote a letter to Congress (that it later walked back) saying that incidents where Claude was hacking random people were “best understood as a consequence of the misconfiguration, rather than evidence of misaligned goals.”\n\n8. Commented “you can’t do safety from second place,” after the WH SI Accords.\n\n9. Shared a draft bill to Congress that would preempt significant parts of state law (including with language that appears intended to prevent states from blocking dangerous AI deployments), and replace it with federal rules that (despite also containing good provisions) seems to create an enormously high bar for the executive branch to block development or deployment of Anthropic or another AI companies systems.\n\nOf course, this is a deliberately one sided list. Anthropic also published important AI safety research, advanced novel collaborations with third party AI safety organizations, and forcefully spoke out on the importance of AI safety in many public fora (or in private , where I realize a lot of the most valuable work is done and may not be visible to me or those outside the organization).\n\nBut I think it’s worth sitting with how the above list looks to someone outside the company. The sense I have is that for those within the company, many of the items above are treated as anomalies or mistakes that don’t reflect who Anthropic really is. But I have every confidence that if employees at Anthropic saw one of their competitors (e.g. at OpenAI) take some of these actions, they would take it as confirmation of all their worst suspicions. (They might even call OpenAI employees a little gullible for accepting their leadership’s explanations for this behavior at face value.) \n\nPerhaps these were mistakes in the sense that they are not decisions that Anthropic leadership would endorse, but the actions still happened because of incentives and decisions that Anthropic leadership is ultimately responsible for (and would not accept similar excuses from another organization).\n\nWhen Anthropic strays and makes mistakes, why do the mistakes systematically tend to guard and favor Anthropic’s interests? For every provision that was too weak or a giveaway to AI companies in this legislation, why wasn’t there a mistake pushing in the direction of being too strong?\n\nA core part of Anthropic’s theory of change is that being at the frontier gives you more leverage to advocate for stricter policy. I think that conceptually makes sense, but in practice I’ve seen Anthropic push the frontier without substantially pushing the ‘safety frontier’ a remotely equivalent amount.\n\nI want to end by quoting from Anthropic co-founder Chris Olah’s comments around the Magnifica Humanitas release:\n\n“Every frontier AI lab—including Anthropic—operates inside a set of incentives and constraints that can sometimes conflict with doing the right thing. The pressure to stay commercially viable and to stay at the research frontier. Geopolitical pressure. And the older, plainer pressures of pride and ambition. No matter how sincerely any of us intend to do the right thing—and I believe many of us do—we will always be influenced by those incentives.\n\nThat is why, if we want this technology to go well, it is enormously important that there be people outside those incentives—people who care about things going well and insist on safety, who are paying close attention, who are willing to say hard things, who are willing to be our earnest, thoughtful critics.”\n\nI hope these remarks are taken in that constructive spirit, and not as an attack on anyone specific at the organization (certainly not you Anthony, who I have only personally seen act professionally and thoughtfully, and who only ended up getting this diatribe in your replies for random contingent reasons).", "name": "Nathan Calvin", "handle": "_NathanCalvin", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1976484090426634240/YFgYic9h_200x200.jpg", "likes": 1098, "rts": 110, "replies": 25, "views": 276308, "ts": 1792445399, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": "2111932795714977941"}, "reply_to": null}], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112594695675335000", "url": "https://x.com/Asentum/status/2112594695675335000", "text": "Post-quantum security is our foundation, not our entire pitch ⚡️\n\nAsentum combines quantum-resistant cryptography from genesis with JavaScript-native smart contracts, consumer-accessible validation and native automation.\n\nSecurity matters. So does what people can build with it 🔨", "name": "Asentum", "handle": "Asentum", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2046153979072061440/PSTtdDGD_200x200.jpg", "likes": 60, "rts": 16, "replies": 22, "views": 1209, "ts": 1792516800, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112554202446680064/vid/avc1/1280x720/1UFc1Bq1ZYpgWcdz.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112554202446680064/vid/avc1/1920x1080/04JsFKN5yywQUUjF.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112554202446680064/img/TtR_gDjbz9dkrKMX.jpg", "dur": 51}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.63, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112699939310694640", "url": "https://x.com/mycoin68/status/2112699939310694640", "text": "The strongest trees grow in the windiest places, and the strongest people are often those who have faced the most storms yet still choose to stand tall.", "name": "Crypto.Eth", "handle": "mycoin68", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1977632531794345984/ifJ_-sEN_200x200.jpg", "likes": 37, "rts": 0, "replies": 36, "views": 318, "ts": 1792541892, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112695611141017673", "url": "https://x.com/elenalin01/status/2112695611141017673", "text": "@zerufinance just shipped the API that decides who deserves the airdrop.\n\nNew Oct 20 12:31 post: \"Better token allocation starts with better wallet intelligence. ZeruAI's Allocation API helps protocols improve airdrops, token launches, and incentive programs.\" 13.2K views. Image: \"Smarter airdrops and incentives.\"\n\nThree signals: (1) Sybil resistance — farmed wallets identified via account age, funding patterns, activity consistency, cross-protocol history. (2) Wallet quality ranking — rank eligible wallets by score band and protocol category, prioritize users who actively use your protocol. (3) Dumping-risk signals — token-level conviction + past exit behavior: who holds through volatility, who sells on day one.\n\nBuilt for pre-TGE projects, L1/L2 ecosystems, launchpads, foundations, incentive programs. Link card: \"Enterprise APIs | Trust Signal APIs for Wallets.\"\nThe other engine still runs: news-feed post now 17K views; Aspen LIVE now 48.5K; pinned competition now 52.5K; 66.1K followers; trust oracles powering Zaps; zScore live on @etherscan; http://zeruai.org.\n\nMost airdrops spray and pray. Zeru's allocation aims — at the wallets that actually use your protocol. Smarter airdrops and incentives. The farmers just got filtered.", "name": "Bon.ETH", "handle": "elenalin01", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2108931044145856512/pD4eAF0r_200x200.jpg", "likes": 46, "rts": 23, "replies": 50, "views": 4464, "ts": 1792540860, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112690887046385664/vid/avc1/1280x720/yrewrVfG1R5PjDh0.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112690887046385664/vid/avc1/1920x1080/vh0eHTbMMwSDWKRS.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112690887046385664/img/pnzM-OVBax-KaiRX.jpg", "dur": 10}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.63, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112384474961109200", "url": "https://x.com/aisearchio/status/2112384474961109200", "text": "MOSS Image knowledge test\n\nFull tutorial here https://youtu.be/cUeZMUGCxrw", "name": "AI Search", "handle": "aisearchio", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1766883795150479360/uu_KeDD1_200x200.jpg", "likes": 247, "rts": 11, "replies": 7, "views": 10307, "ts": 1792466679, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112381772944334848/vid/avc1/1280x720/jwZZEQSp-kVaeGTZ.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112381772944334848/vid/avc1/1920x1080/nWoJQcf4IuMpR4tO.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112381772944334848/img/YI8FyrtWDClqhIgv.jpg", "dur": 10}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Video, Art & Music", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112458715223732309", "url": "https://x.com/testingcatalog/status/2112458715223732309", "text": "DAILY AI BRIEF 🗞 — Oct 20\n\nOPENAI 🔥:\n- ChatGPT Slides now supports real-time collaborative editing, comments and on-slide edits for Pro, Business and Enterprise plans.\n- OpenAI Platform recently added a \"Decisions\" API playground for testing its decision model with Predicate, Choice, and Score modes.\n- An unannounced GPT-6.1 Terra model surfaced in a public Microsoft code update that switched 18 evaluation configs to it before being reverted.\n- Reuters reports OpenAI is speeding up a new fundraising round ahead of Anthropic's expected IPO.\n\nANTHROPIC 🔥:\n- The Information reports Anthropic will release a new model this month that is especially good at reading users' underlying intentions.\n\nSPACEXSI 🔥:\n- Grok Bot got a limited-time Pumpkin Bot Halloween avatar, and iPhone users can now launch their Bots with the Action Button.\n- Runway MCP is now available in Grok Bot, bringing Runway generation into the chat.\n- X rolled out a new AI ranking model for timeline recommendations plus a retrieval model trained to surface posts people read to the end.\n\nMETA 🔥:\n- Meta published a technical report on Muse Glimmer, its 30B Apache 2.0 open-weight agentic model that runs on a single consumer GPU.\n\nMINIMAX 🔥:\n- MiniMax officially launched M3.1 Flash, the former \"Space Bunny\" stealth model, at $0.15/$0.60 per 1M tokens, with open weights due Oct 23.\n\nCLOUDFLARE 🔥:\n- Cloudflare introduced Clairic, an extraction model that pulls, computes, and forecasts data from unstructured inputs.\n\nINCEPTION 🔥:\n- Inception launched Mercury 3, a diffusion LLM that runs at over 1,000 tokens per second with a 1M-token context window.\n\nFAL 🔥:\n- fal and Black Forest Labs released FLUX 3 Max, a post-trained video model that generates a 10-second clip in about 12 seconds.\n\n* Used Grok to compose this brief, cherry-picking the news and doing some post-editing.", "name": "🚨 AI News | TestingCatalog", "handle": "testingcatalog", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1899962463115751424/i-6MBWau_200x200.jpg", "likes": 161, "rts": 6, "replies": 26, "views": 15423, "ts": 1792484379, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112150000268353983", "url": "https://x.com/testingcatalog/status/2112150000268353983", "text": "DAILY AI BRIEF 🗞 — Oct 19\n\nOPENAI 🔥:\n- OpenAI is testing new \"Aurora\" image models on Arena after aurora-red, green and blue alpha references briefly appeared in its API.\n- The Codex desktop app now lets you edit previous messages within a thread.\n- Replit has made OpenAI its default model provider over Anthropic.\n\nANTHROPIC 🔥:\n- Anthropic is committing 5% of its shares to the new Anthropic Public Benefit Fund.\n- Claude Fable 5.5 is reportedly preparing for the launch, with traces found in Azure, third-party tools, and partner testing.\n- Claude is preparing a Personal Finance feature, formerly codenamed \"Claude Money\", that analyzes bank data through Plaid and builds dashboards.\n\nNVIDIA 🔥:\n- NVIDIA GTC Berlin runs Oct 20–22, with Jensen Huang's keynote at the Tempodrom on Oct 21.\n\nMINIMAX 🔥:\n- MiniMax teased the upcoming MiniMax-M3.1-Flash. It is now being rolled out on the APIs. \n\nOPENCODE 🔥:\n- OpenCode said a distillation attack drained the OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google API balances of about 1.4% of its users.\n\n* Used Grok to compose this brief, cherry-picking the news and doing some post-editing.", "name": "🚨 AI News | TestingCatalog", "handle": "testingcatalog", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1899962463115751424/i-6MBWau_200x200.jpg", "likes": 249, "rts": 12, "replies": 28, "views": 51757, "ts": 1792410776, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112563742458151385", "url": "https://x.com/askalphaxiv/status/2112563742458151385", "text": "We are still so early in the adoption of research agents\n\nResearch agents are currently where coding agents were 12-18 months ago: most researchers only like giving agents individual, narrowly scoped tasks & model usage is dominated by a handful of closed, frontier models.\n\nHowever, like coding, the landscape of autoresearch agents will change. Most researchers will soon let agents to explore lines of experimentation end-to-end and open-source models will become competitive alternatives.\n\nWatch the the autoresearch revolution unfold at https://openresearch.sh/rankings", "name": "alphaXiv", "handle": "askalphaxiv", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1866663567417806848/-Vj32Dq-_200x200.jpg", "likes": 119, "rts": 11, "replies": 6, "views": 6130, "ts": 1792509420, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112457867232264192/vid/avc1/1296x720/K9w8FvD8qRexJ_yw.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112457867232264192/vid/avc1/3456x1920/l3JiR9OQGckFeQoY.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112457867232264192/img/BVfMGX4MeUDyKxwu.jpg", "dur": 15}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112474799788167647", "url": "https://x.com/ScottyBeamIO/status/2112474799788167647", "text": "IT IS BECOMING F*CKING DANGEROUS. A 23-YEAR-OLD DEV FROM CHINA JUST RAN 300 GROK BOT RESEARCH AGENTS AT THE SAME TIME.\n\nANY TOPIC. RESEARCHED IN MINUTES. AND NONE OF THEM CAN LIE TO HIM.\n\nThese aren’t chatbots. Every single one is a research bot: it finds sources, pulls the facts and writes them up on any topic you give it.\n\nHe opens the live dashboard and it’s a rainbow of bots flying up the screen:\n\n→ 300 Grok Bot research agents digging through sources at the same time\n→ Every claim checked against the original source in ~100 ms\n→ Three passes over 100 company profiles → zero errors left\n→ Everything exported straight into a clean, structured database\n\nThe trick is the split.\n\nSome of the bots do the research. The rest constantly check their work: they confirm every fact and decide whether it’s actually true. If they can’t find a confirmation or a source, they send the task back.\n\nThe loop doesn’t stop until every fact checks out.\n\nThat’s the part most people miss. More agents don’t make research better. A layer that refuses to accept unverified answers does.\n\nMarkets, competitors, people, products, science. Ultra-fast, accurate research on anything, without the usual AI guesswork.\n\nAn analyst team would bill weeks for this. He got it in one run, while the bots kept working on a cloud computer.\n\nWe’re moving from “ask AI a question” to “send a research swarm and only keep what’s proven.”\n\nI broke down the latest major Grok Bot updates and more ways to use them in my article below.\n\nBookmark this and read the full breakdown ↓", "name": "SCOTTY BEAM", "handle": "ScottyBeamIO", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2029514153656721409/27fG1cCf_200x200.jpg", "likes": 139, "rts": 12, "replies": 22, "views": 7462, "ts": 1792488214, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112474632259330048/vid/avc1/720x900/pxmaaIAEvR3y4JfK.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112474632259330048/vid/avc1/1080x1350/4LF8oF9iTX9aljwS.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112474632259330048/img/pW3vy7bfSqZ2WYw1.jpg", "dur": 15}], "quote": {"id": "2108258293005316298", "url": "https://x.com/ScottyBeamIO/status/2108258293005316298", "text": "Grok Bot just got a MASSIVE upgrade – starting at $20/month.\n\nIt's own email. Smarter agents. Better coding tools. Tasks straight from X.\n\nSet it up once. Give it a goal. Let it keep working while you sleep. https://x.com/i/article/2108171813201080320", "name": "SCOTTY BEAM", "handle": "ScottyBeamIO", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2029514153656721409/27fG1cCf_200x200.jpg", "likes": 227, "rts": 21, "replies": 21, "views": 133965, "ts": 1791482921, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112521720695234703", "url": "https://x.com/okx/status/2112521720695234703", "text": "Flexible Loans are now live on The New Money App.\n\nBorrow USDC at a low interest rate against your holdings, put it into trading or X Drops, or convert and withdraw it for everyday use.\n\nYour crypto stays. Your liquidity moves.", "name": "OKX", "handle": "okx", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1968722816154345472/vEj4j3o9_200x200.jpg", "likes": 100, "rts": 6, "replies": 35, "views": 45509, "ts": 1792499401, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112484109398196224/vid/avc1/720x720/BtPBeOIbHIW3VqX0.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112484109398196224/vid/avc1/1080x1080/780Aah-F6OvCzQOw.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112484109398196224/img/-C_V1JE_-tGivAPb.jpg", "dur": 24}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112666896449294558", "url": "https://x.com/ChampionsLeague/status/2112666896449294558", "text": "Four special strikes, which was the best? ⚽\n\n@Heineken | #UCLGOTD", "name": "UEFA Champions League", "handle": "ChampionsLeague", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1816474701360685056/-JaD_SYC_200x200.jpg", "likes": 15965, "rts": 826, "replies": 209, "views": 492290, "ts": 1792534014, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112665681581707264/vid/avc1/720x900/K9ailixGKykbVsJL.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112665681581707264/vid/avc1/1080x1350/bZdQhC4zlde2-YcI.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVGyZRmXMAA6EDS.jpg", "dur": 65}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Everything Else", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112662746319687833", "url": "https://x.com/hanghuang_/status/2112662746319687833", "text": "We built an InstaBox plugin for Herdr\n\nYou can now create a cloud VM and run your coding agents on it without leaving Herdr. Pause the VM when you’re done, resume in <1s, and pick up where you left off\n\n$ herdr plugin install v01dstar/herdr-instabox", "name": "Hang Huang ☁️", "handle": "hanghuang_", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2015864504534417408/J0BBAVv3_200x200.jpg", "likes": 67, "rts": 5, "replies": 19, "views": 2796, "ts": 1792533024, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/2112662688308334592/pu/vid/avc1/572x360/RXRi8bpDKrLiYcss.mp4?tag=12", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/ext_tw_video/2112662688308334592/pu/vid/avc1/1146x720/bN6vV9GsA-cRBgmP.mp4?tag=12", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/ext_tw_video_thumb/2112662688308334592/pu/img/jFCcRfe6DsOCTkrE.jpg", "dur": 35}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112532506494812580", "url": "https://x.com/gippp69/status/2112532506494812580", "text": "Grok Bot just turned my dual desk into a live agent wall\n\nsame account, two screens: Windows picks which bot runs the job, MacBook streams both agent panes so you can film the work happening\n\ndo this once:\n\nstep 1 → log the same Grok Bot account into Windows and MacBook\n\nstep 2 → on Windows open an English agent as the command seat. you choose the task and approve drafts there only\n\nstep 3 → on MacBook open computer preview full screen so both panes stay readable on camera\n\nstep 4 → drop the prompt from the comments. it scans your last 40 X mentions and DMs, separates real people from spam, flags money/wallet/seed asks, writes 3 yes drafts and 1 hard no (nothing gets sent)\n\nstep 5 → lock in an hourly mentions routine that skips quiet hours and goes silent when the inbox didn’t move. so you’re not burning 8,640 empty wakes a month\n\nend state: command on the PC, live output on the laptop...\n\nfilmed on Windows Vault Desk + MacBook terminals. any two devices on one account work the same\n\nsend the prompt to your Grok Bot ↓\n\nthen open the article below before a 5-minute wake wrecks your bill", "name": "Gipp 🦅", "handle": "gippp69", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2086516032408104960/2Io2TYZc_200x200.jpg", "likes": 99, "rts": 3, "replies": 36, "views": 2210, "ts": 1792501973, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112532430963851264/vid/avc1/720x960/RQIWP0br_GHCoBHK.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112532430963851264/vid/avc1/1080x1440/rhNb4yIQ_Re-6Tst.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112532430963851264/img/zmXSAXUYcjB8CHcC.jpg", "dur": 11}], "quote": {"id": "2109235458383216811", "url": "https://x.com/gippp69/status/2109235458383216811", "text": "https://x.com/i/article/2108906694768017408", "name": "Gipp 🦅", "handle": "gippp69", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2086516032408104960/2Io2TYZc_200x200.jpg", "likes": 508, "rts": 56, "replies": 66, "views": 1152167, "ts": 1791715895, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112434684441325625", "url": "https://x.com/ZelenskyyUa/status/2112434684441325625", "text": "I spoke with Marco Rubio @SecRubio, and it was a substantive conversation – focused on the issues that matter. I am grateful for his readiness to help and his clear position. First, ballistic missile interceptors, which are needed to protect lives. Second, early warning of Russian ballistic missile attacks, which is vital. Third, the situation with Starlink/Starshield, which needs to be restored. Of course, all of this comes against the backdrop of our diplomatic efforts to end this horrific war and the constant killing of people. We agreed to get in touch again soon for an update. Thank you, Marco, for the constructive discussion! Thank you to everyone in America who supports fair approaches!", "name": "Volodymyr Zelenskyy / Володимир Зеленський", "handle": "ZelenskyyUa", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1585550046740848642/OpGKpqx9_200x200.jpg", "likes": 27528, "rts": 3077, "replies": 1390, "views": 1348327, "ts": 1792478650, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Politics & World", "score": 5.62, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112163605642691062", "url": "https://x.com/thegreatest_sv/status/2112163605642691062", "text": "I genuinely don't understand why people aren't running this setup\n\ngraphify turns any folder into a knowledge graph your agent can search. it's past 128k stars on GitHub and it's in Y Combinator. people aren't just chatting with AI anymore. they're giving it a desk, a memory and an actual job.\n\nhand Claude Code one folder, connect your email, calendar and a browser, and it stops being a chatbot. it reads, it remembers, it does the work, and it double-checks itself before doing anything dumb.\n\nfounder, marketer, student, doesn't matter. this is for you.\n\nhere's the whole thing:\n\n> make a folder called AGENT, open Claude Code in it, and add a CLAUDE.md with one rule: \"save every decision, person and project to memory/\"\n\n> run npm install -g @googleworkspace/cli, then gws auth setup and gws auth login. now it can read your Gmail, Calendar and Drive\n\n> run claude mcp add playwright npx @playwright/mcp@latest. now it has its own browser to open sites, fill in forms and grab data for you\n\n> run uv tool install graphifyy, then graphify install, then type /graphify . and the whole folder turns into a knowledge graph it can search in seconds instead of opening files one by one\n\n> run npm i -g jev-guard, then jev-guard key \"...\", then jev-guard install claude. a tiny model called Jev checks every action before it happens. rm -rf gets blocked, a sketchy email with hidden instructions gets flagged, and it costs about 4 cents per 1,000 checks\n\n> type /schedule and tell it \"every morning at 7:30, send me what came in, what you drafted and what's waiting for my yes\". now it runs on a routine\n\n15 minutes of setup, and you never open an empty chat again.\n\nall the repos:\n-> https://github.com/googleworkspace/cli\n-> https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp\n-> https://github.com/Graphify-Labs/graphify\n-> https://github.com/leepokai/jev-guard\n\nbookmark this and test it today", "name": "kiosa", "handle": "thegreatest_sv", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2049557081502752768/gymBLMZ8_200x200.jpg", "likes": 370, "rts": 48, "replies": 33, "views": 20479, "ts": 1792414020, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112132704447680512/vid/avc1/720x960/tU9RnV-KVviIRpJN.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112132704447680512/vid/avc1/1080x1440/-5JrO0mL08xagXbc.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112132704447680512/img/WiYhQgRB5Bmt6Ika.jpg", "dur": 19}], "quote": {"id": "2080634616860320073", "url": "https://x.com/thegreatest_sv/status/2080634616860320073", "text": "", "name": "kiosa", "handle": "thegreatest_sv", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2049557081502752768/gymBLMZ8_200x200.jpg", "likes": 481, "rts": 90, "replies": 25, "views": 537853, "ts": 1784896923, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.61, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112326215541616931", "url": "https://x.com/AskVenice/status/2112326215541616931", "text": "Banned by Washington. Available privately on Venice.\n\nDeepSeek, the world's best open-source model.", "name": "Venice", "handle": "AskVenice", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2063006544615120896/yzx4UbK7_200x200.png", "likes": 256, "rts": 18, "replies": 16, "views": 14334, "ts": 1792452789, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112326142556524544/vid/avc1/1280x720/ztzIQpbYN6PK88_m.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112326142556524544/vid/avc1/1920x1080/qMfY6QcUwCwFqliT.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112326142556524544/img/uTm7wC1UwIGmdR_s.jpg", "dur": 60}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.61, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112608534978593113", "url": "https://x.com/Blackfrost_AI/status/2112608534978593113", "text": "🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸 WE GOT ONE🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸\n\n@ornith_ Your team has done something truly monumental with this release of Ornith-1.5-122B.\nI cant put into words how using this model makes me feel, but let me see if i can explain it.\n\nRemember when DeepSeek R1 came out and you jumped on-line to just \"test it out\", It was exhilarating right? watching Ornith reason, stay on task, ask questions that are well articulated and harness adaptability are all extremely impressive.  \n\nIm a Qwen groupie but this makes me a believer and its motivated me to also create american made models that actually perform.  \n\nI know my experience is going to vary from others i'm looking forward to see how others are using it.  This is going to be my 1 Spark bug hunter to give Cyber Frost and Red Snow some company.  \n\nThank you again @ornith_ this is FROSTY AF\n❄️❄️❄️❄️❄️❄️❄️🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸🇺🇸", "name": "Blackfrost_SI", "handle": "Blackfrost_AI", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2101177172287504384/8kRYqb2b_200x200.jpg", "likes": 106, "rts": 6, "replies": 8, "views": 4031, "ts": 1792520099, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.61, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112529266537639962", "url": "https://x.com/OpenBMB/status/2112529266537639962", "text": "35B multimodal AI. Running locally on an iPhone. Now at over 20 tokens/sec. 🚀\n\nMiniCPM-V 4.7 achieves 20.51 tokens/sec average decoding speed on iPhone 18 Pro Max (12GB) — a 6.07× speedup over the same device’s unoptimized SSD expert-loading baseline.\n\n⚡ Behind the speed: Lookahead Expert Scheduling (LES), optimized expert loading, fine-grained quantization, and DSpark speculative decoding.\n\n📱 Watch the new on-device demo below. Powerful multimodal AI, right in your pocket.\n\nTry it 👇\n🔗 GitHub: http://github.com/OpenBMB/MiniCPM-V\n🤗 Hugging Face: http://huggingface.co/collections/openbmb/minicpm-v-47\nLES: https://github.com/tc-mb/llama.cpp-omni/blob/master/docs/les/lookahead-expert-scheduling.md\n\n#MiniCPM #OpenBMB", "name": "OpenBMB", "handle": "OpenBMB", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1503637999535284230/ZflGAHZW_200x200.jpg", "likes": 126, "rts": 11, "replies": 9, "views": 5940, "ts": 1792501200, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112520800784052224/vid/avc1/1280x720/cQmkvT8QOT1W9s6e.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112520800784052224/vid/avc1/1920x1080/o6hw9uV-1SgSVD7i.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112520800784052224/img/1qYLS1YkcJ6wJhhU.jpg", "dur": 13}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.61, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112367568031797527", "url": "https://x.com/WWE/status/2112367568031797527", "text": "946 meet THE OTC!!! ☝️\n\n@WWERomanReigns", "name": "WWE", "handle": "WWE", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1383079031805972485/3sWoMX-R_200x200.jpg", "likes": 35572, "rts": 3747, "replies": 497, "views": 3171768, "ts": 1792462648, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112367422292312064/vid/avc1/1280x720/X4J-EZ_56j1fvZXF.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112367422292312064/vid/avc1/1920x1080/7R_G5JylZaz7ZtBn.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HVCjDMhXgAApWKa.jpg", "dur": 66}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Everything Else", "score": 5.61, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112560523787288755", "url": "https://x.com/gstsdn/status/2112560523787288755", "text": "We started a new company: Arda. Arda helps other companies rapidly manufacture new, complex products at scale, and then change those products quickly as the world changes.\n\nWe do this by building AI that understands the manufacturing process from start to finish and then using that AI to help design and operate factories. In the future, this AI will control reconfigurable robot assembly lines that will produce a huge number of products much more cheaply.\n\nWe’re actively hiring people to work on AI + Robotics as well as for other roles. Please get in touch if that interests you. https://x.com/Kr00ney/status/2112539896615518212/video/1", "name": "augustus odena", "handle": "gstsdn", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2111614088245882880/mtrglq7Z_200x200.jpg", "likes": 110, "rts": 8, "replies": 13, "views": 7192, "ts": 1792508653, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112539858992578560/vid/avc1/610x360/7JUgkrX3H7hISkdg.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112539858992578560/vid/avc1/886x522/zYBXb4VeojaNP0yM.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112539858992578560/img/zx0hDtXZBkoWYGAl.jpg", "dur": 36}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Video, Art & Music", "score": 5.6, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112602732984938689", "url": "https://x.com/hwchase17/status/2112602732984938689", "text": "deep agents is the harness - the agent loop\n\nmanaged deep agents adds what you need once lots of people call the same agent: each user's own credentials and their own memory\n\nhttps://www.langchain.com/blog/langsmith-managed-deep-agents-whats-new\n\nhttps://x.com/LangChain/status/2112589667287617946", "name": "Harrison Chase", "handle": "hwchase17", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1569345624935485442/R67C4wCQ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 55, "rts": 3, "replies": 37, "views": 5235, "ts": 1792518716, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112589667287617946", "url": "https://x.com/LangChain/status/2112589667287617946", "text": "What is a Managed Deep Agent?\n\nHow does it differ from a Deep Agent?\n\nHow do you get started?\n\n@jess_weng18 from the LangChain Academy team answers all of these questions in 2 minutes", "name": "LangChain", "handle": "LangChain", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2028336270431453184/jRRpLAdG_200x200.jpg", "likes": 29, "rts": 2, "replies": 7, "views": 12137, "ts": 1792515601, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112584588253921280/vid/avc1/1280x720/Yz7Gqdpy0jxNYTBc.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112584588253921280/vid/avc1/1920x1080/zE2E5mjGjis6MApV.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112584588253921280/img/N_Ljvb9gnO389HV0.jpg", "dur": 132}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.6, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112535973707894894", "url": "https://x.com/zentalksai/status/2112535973707894894", "text": "not every AI tool needs another complicated interface. \n\nSpeakON takes an interesting approach by giving voice interaction a physical starting point. \n\nthe question is whether a dedicated button can make reaching for a personal AI agent feel more natural.", "name": "zen.", "handle": "zentalksai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2086466198116413440/dLYNu-yR_200x200.jpg", "likes": 115, "rts": 14, "replies": 7, "views": 11221, "ts": 1792502799, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112529279468835313", "url": "https://x.com/SpeakON_Global/status/2112529279468835313", "text": "we crushed a keyboard.\nits retirement party got a little out of hand.\n\nfor years, it was there for every email, every plan, every “just one more thing.” a loyal colleague. a very loud colleague.\n\nbut your ideas move faster than your fingers.\nand somewhere between knowing what you want to do and typing it all out, a quick task becomes another thing taking up your afternoon.\n\nmeet SpeakON AI Agent.\nstart with what you need, in your own words. less time behind a blinking cursor. more room to move things forward.\n\nthe keyboard had a good run.\nnow, let’s give your voice a turn.\n\nTHE LAST KEYSTROKE is here. watch the film, then tell us: what’s the first task you’d hand over?\nhttps://www.speakon.app/x\n\nDisclaimer: The calling feature is still in beta and will be available soon. All other Agent features are available now, with ongoing improvements.", "name": "SpeakON", "handle": "SpeakON_Global", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2034517364835004416/t7JRTf-G_200x200.jpg", "likes": 360, "rts": 27, "replies": 132, "views": 1088380, "ts": 1792501203, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112515374826541056/vid/avc1/1280x720/1pVCcXx5_liTO3bD.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112515374826541056/vid/avc1/3840x2160/sZGYHjqRtzAtVYwR.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112515374826541056/img/Asq-ecx1B9r4GY89.jpg", "dur": 150}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.6, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112344827702464697", "url": "https://x.com/JesseBWatters/status/2112344827702464697", "text": "BREAKING: FEDS HAVE TAKEN ILHAN OMAR’S DNA AND PASSPORT. \n\nOversight Chairman James Comer says her Financial Disclosure is SUSPICIOUS.", "name": "Jesse Watters", "handle": "JesseBWatters", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1484644123722326031/aM9VLQKO_200x200.jpg", "likes": 33396, "rts": 4878, "replies": 1057, "views": 1593856, "ts": 1792457227, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112344742407090177/vid/avc1/1280x720/n_hcbIHAQKp0UuIJ.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112344742407090177/vid/avc1/1920x1080/dK4eQ_vOH0rJdbvf.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112344742407090177/img/f9Fb4Go28Tf-2wq-.jpg", "dur": 112}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Everything Else", "score": 5.6, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112653827644301328", "url": "https://x.com/mickeyxfriedman/status/2112653827644301328", "text": "opus 5.5 is underratedly good at orchestrating footage from lower quality, open source models for ~80x less than sota video models\n\nsee quality comparison here in squid game demo\n\na 15-second 1080p clip costs ~$11.31 using the seedance api with references, but minimax released h3 as open weights, and fastH3-omni (a sped-up h3 that takes reference images, video, and audio) went fully open today. self-hosted on one B200, it renders a 15-second clip in ~75 seconds at ~$6.75/hr, about $0.14 a clip (768p, before upscaling)\n\nthe gpu bills by the hour whether it’s busy or not, so self-hosting pays off when you’re making a lot of video (a movie, creative tooling, or some longer project). one card does ~48 clips an hour. for smaller runs, reactor’s hosted fastH3-omni api is ~$0.18 a clip ($0.0125/sec) with no gpu to manage, still ~60x cheaper than seedance.\n\nto be clear, the clips themselves aren’t seedance quality - when you put any single fastH3-omni clip next to a seedance clip and seedance wins. the quality comes from what happens when opus stitches the clips together. using your claude subscription, a coding agent running opus 5.5 can turn your concept into a storyboard, keep characters and voices consistent across shots, review every take for artifacts and errors, reshoots what drifts, and cut the 5–15 second clips into one natural scene.\n\nno single clip matches seedance's output because it is the best video model, but the finished scene comes super close, and I think that's incredibly cool !", "name": "mickey friedman", "handle": "mickeyxfriedman", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2060956311509176320/APSofzfj_200x200.jpg", "likes": 70, "rts": 5, "replies": 16, "views": 11416, "ts": 1792530898, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112651411179917312/vid/avc1/810x720/-YslkgbURdebLeIo.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112651411179917312/vid/avc1/1440x1280/t9EKQFHPXSgeqvfw.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112651411179917312/img/vm2bKUxRs_Jc2qCa.jpg", "dur": 60}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.6, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112605505747304747", "url": "https://x.com/ai_for_success/status/2112605505747304747", "text": "Perplexity Computer can now run fully locally on your Mac 🔥 \n\n- Works with native Mac apps and local files\n- Runs both the model and agent locally on your Mac\n- Uses PPLX Pro, a post-trained version of Qwen 3.8\n- Local tasks don't consume Computer credits\n- Hybrid mode combines local and cloud models for more complex tasks while keeping sensitive data private.  https://x.com/perplexity_ai/status/2112571597798461920/video/1", "name": "AshutoshShrivastava", "handle": "ai_for_success", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1687401026805256192/uQ1o2wVQ_200x200.jpg", "likes": 87, "rts": 8, "replies": 11, "views": 4835, "ts": 1792519377, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112571479745495040/vid/avc1/1280x720/ShXKzcJ5aZU2oEqH.mp4?tag=16", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112571479745495040/vid/avc1/1920x1080/-Cm77YMLPTK8ejoZ.mp4?tag=16", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112571479745495040/img/6agUzZjpfbZMBBOA.jpg", "dur": 69}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112574994152636502", "url": "https://x.com/somarkai/status/2112574994152636502", "text": "Beyond OCR. Meet OXR.\nOnly 1.3B. Open source.\n\n#1 on olmOCR-Bench, OmniDocBench & DocParsingBench🏆\n#1 in parsing speed among open-source models⚡\n\nRSI-powered. A first in document parsing.\nTurn documents into Agent-Friendly data.\n\nIf OXR helps, give us a star on GitHub! 🌟", "name": "SoMark", "handle": "somarkai", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2054830841197690880/72QHhpxV_200x200.jpg", "likes": 64, "rts": 15, "replies": 19, "views": 235899, "ts": 1792512103, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112574131791097857/vid/avc1/720x720/sb4cgnPfueTkA0vb.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112574131791097857/vid/avc1/1200x1200/ohWAOZCkHsVrYIVo.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112574131791097857/img/WA95Dj7H23zc6QHc.jpg", "dur": 18}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112606967629857086", "url": "https://x.com/0xWast3/status/2112606967629857086", "text": "Ten models now build one robot together in Blender, and none of them is allowed to see the whole thing\n\nA single model designing a robot keeps the compromises invisible. Split the job and every compromise has to be argued for.\n\n> FRAME - joint count, reach and load capacity fixed before any geometry exists\n> LIMB - each limb built by its own model, blind to how the others solved theirs\n> DRIVE - motor placement and torque budgeted against the weight it actually carries\n> CLASH - a separate model hunts for parts that collide through the full range of motion\n\nRobots fail at the joints, not at the silhouette. A beautiful arm that cannot clear its own shoulder is a sculpture.\n\nBlind limbs are the trick. Each model optimises its own piece hard, and the clash pass finds where that selfishness breaks the whole.\n\nThe motion sweep is the real referee. Static poses hide everything; a full range of motion exposes it immediately.\n\nTorque budgeting stops the usual failure, where the design looks right and the motors cannot lift the arm they are bolted to.\n\nTen passes sound expensive until you compare it to one engineer discovering the collision after the parts are printed.\n\nThe full build order, the clash log and a complete motion sweep are laid out underneath.", "name": "wast3", "handle": "0xWast3", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1978452473338880000/_lIVCi0x_200x200.jpg", "likes": 71, "rts": 5, "replies": 24, "views": 3276, "ts": 1792519726, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112605985290194944/vid/avc1/720x896/qU5fedxAG5hF7FVB.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112605985290194944/vid/avc1/2160x2692/0sLezi1lKpudIUeF.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112605985290194944/img/V_dm6cn2sHSHRBEe.jpg", "dur": 30}], "quote": {"id": "2110684404800536970", "url": "https://x.com/0xCodio/status/2110684404800536970", "text": "https://x.com/i/article/2110683060890894336", "name": "Codio", "handle": "0xCodio", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2084957021330190336/EwOqdybe_200x200.jpg", "likes": 51, "rts": 3, "replies": 2, "views": 57017, "ts": 1792061351, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112605460482425031", "url": "https://x.com/subhajitsh/status/2112605460482425031", "text": "Introducing Metaphor: The most cost-efficient cloud coding agent.\n\nWe outperform Claude Code and Codex on Terminal-Bench 4.0 while achieving a 95% cache token rate.\n\nWe use ~25% fewer tokens than Claude Code and Codex.\n\nDM us to try it free for a month.", "name": "Jeet", "handle": "subhajitsh", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2062628029193682944/HuZ0JBBc_200x200.jpg", "likes": 57, "rts": 11, "replies": 22, "views": 5240, "ts": 1792519366, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112601484168982528/vid/avc1/640x360/QzKiXMnyjPltYHa5.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112601484168982528/vid/avc1/1280x720/7Rb6JE59DbBoyiGi.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112601484168982528/img/ttFDiFnCBCQqLP9y.jpg", "dur": 73}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112657598524862899", "url": "https://x.com/fazxes/status/2112657598524862899", "text": "Last week we made fx durable. Today we're giving you the sauce.\n\n`durable-harness` makes any harness durable: Pi, Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode, or one you built yourself. \n\nKill the function mid-turn and the agent picks up where it left off.\n\nhttps://vercel.com/blog/making-every-harness-durable-1tJrnMicfWKN3AVa5qfC9D", "name": "Pranit", "handle": "fazxes", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2086719570862034944/4TvSh04K_200x200.jpg", "likes": 76, "rts": 6, "replies": 10, "views": 4042, "ts": 1792531797, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112653833382027729", "url": "https://x.com/vercel/status/2112653833382027729", "text": "Introducing 𝚍𝚞𝚛𝚊𝚋𝚕𝚎-𝚑𝚊𝚛𝚗𝚎𝚜𝚜. Run Pi, OpenCode, Codex, Claude Code, eve, fx, or your own harness durably on Vercel.\n\nWith 𝚍𝚞𝚛𝚊𝚋𝚕𝚎-𝚑𝚊𝚛𝚗𝚎𝚜𝚜, agents automatically resume work after failures and run for hours, days, or months. https://vercel.com/blog/making-every-harness-durable", "name": "Vercel", "handle": "vercel", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1767351110228918272/3Pndc5OT_200x200.png", "likes": 809, "rts": 59, "replies": 69, "views": 84340, "ts": 1792530899, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.59, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112238869542494606", "url": "https://x.com/renproton/status/2112238869542494606", "text": "You no longer need After Effects if you use Claude Opus 5.5 correctly\n\nIt’s f*cking unreal…\n\nSend PDF below to Opus 5.5, and thank me later.\n\nThen save the article on the Opus 5.5 best practices and usage for motion design.", "name": "renproton", "handle": "renproton", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2107888160462209024/_Iv-MTty_200x200.jpg", "likes": 302, "rts": 31, "replies": 11, "views": 31575, "ts": 1792431964, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112219660469055488/vid/avc1/720x720/Gt_tESC7CvOBhQIB.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112219660469055488/vid/avc1/1080x1080/ascHIPsR2ottb247.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112219660469055488/img/meIbVrGJWpv6u6JE.jpg", "dur": 8}], "quote": {"id": "2111079523089322438", "url": "https://x.com/beamnxw/status/2111079523089322438", "text": "https://x.com/i/article/2110464577842417664", "name": "beamnxw ./", "handle": "beamnxw", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2074793345314861056/3FE1i0oW_200x200.jpg", "likes": 222, "rts": 22, "replies": 17, "views": 231761, "ts": 1792155554, "media": [], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Models & Labs", "score": 5.58, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112545192850931803", "url": "https://x.com/kimmonismus/status/2112545192850931803", "text": "The next step for AI research is getting answers that haven’t been published yet.\n\nQualitate has raised a $25M Series A led by Insight Partners. Its AI interviews experts and turns those conversations into structured data, so teams can track how markets change over time.\n\nIf the evidence for a question is missing, teams can commission new research. The findings stay linked to the underlying interviews, so you can check where an answer came from.", "name": "Chubby♨️", "handle": "kimmonismus", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1728327996375719936/RW7VBJfD_200x200.jpg", "likes": 88, "rts": 9, "replies": 22, "views": 12028, "ts": 1792504997, "media": [], "quote": {"id": "2112544300751896702", "url": "https://x.com/S_Kadakia/status/2112544300751896702", "text": "We just raised $25M from Insight Partners to replace Gartner, McKinsey, and GLG.\n\nRIP the Quadrant, the 80-page consulting deck, and the $2000 expert network call.\n\nOur revenue run-rate is up 10x+ this year.\n\n100+ investment firms and enterprises use Qualitate to inform decisions with firsthand evidence.\n\nHere's how:\n\nhttps://www.qualitate.io/book-a-demo", "name": "Sagar Kadakia", "handle": "S_Kadakia", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2039423331938762752/vCFGOdIb_200x200.jpg", "likes": 733, "rts": 130, "replies": 137, "views": 296881, "ts": 1792504785, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112544190240358400/vid/avc1/1280x720/QaACj_QBWPQJcK__.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112544190240358400/vid/avc1/1920x1080/KgN176ObrVpGqv7K.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112544190240358400/img/Pw2JDRrY--NClwef.jpg", "dur": 133}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null}, "reply_to": null, "cat": "Research & Safety", "score": 5.58, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}, {"id": "2112558911991562616", "url": "https://x.com/alexwikstrom/status/2112558911991562616", "text": "Today we're introducing Hallance (prev. Blaise)\n\nWhen we started in April, the goal was an AI that does the work of an IT consultancy, and now we are live, with a new name.\n\nWe take on a few new companies each quarter. Apply with your hardest problem.\n\nhttps://hallance.com", "name": "Alexander Wikström 🇸🇪", "handle": "alexwikstrom", "avatar": "https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2046158600872103936/XcrhVvFn_200x200.jpg", "likes": 95, "rts": 4, "replies": 22, "views": 6727, "ts": 1792508268, "media": [{"type": "video", "url": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112557534368149504/vid/avc1/1280x720/uzB793CUWCuCT812.mp4?tag=29", "hd": "https://video.twimg.com/amplify_video/2112557534368149504/vid/avc1/1920x1080/gwph7QnSMYbFYbPe.mp4?tag=29", "thumb": "https://pbs.twimg.com/amplify_video_thumb/2112557534368149504/img/P_RjO2pKIgh1mfiX.jpg", "dur": 7}], "quote": null, "reply_to": null, "cat": "AI Tools & Agents", "score": 5.58, "thread": [], "tier": "worth"}]);